// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET
//
// Registry #930 — a blanket method's SET bound, at a METHOD call.
//
// `export fn[S Ints] S @to_i16()` (`std/src/prelude/protocols.nv`) is bounded to
// the `Ints` type-set. `f64` is not one of its members. Until 2026-09-05 the bound was
// enforced at a free-function call and NOWHERE else: measured to the end before the
// fix, `(2.5 as f64).to_i16()` compiled, ran, and printed `true` — a lie returned
// by the one call a user makes in order to be TOLD the truth about a conversion.
//
// Same skeleton as #894 and #921: one rule, one of its two entrances unguarded. The
// control for that claim is not prose — it is `p930_blanket_set_bound_freefn_neg.nv`,
// the same premise through the door that has always checked, which must keep giving
// this same diagnostic.
//
// Scope is PRIMITIVE receivers, deliberately, because that is where the other door
// stops too: `only_ints(Count(5))` for `type Count int` compiles clean there. Making
// the method door stricter would rebuild the very asymmetry this row removes. Whether
// a newtype belongs to its carrier's set is an owner question — D310 says
// explicit-member-set only, the compiler already lets a `Count` flow into `int`
// positions — and it is filed separately rather than settled here.
//
// Spec: D310 (`spec/decisions/02-types.md`, "Type-set bounds"), bound = membership
// predicate. Positive half: `spec_tests/conformance/p930_blanket_set_bound_pos.nv`.
module neg.p930_blanket_set_bound_f64_neg

fn ask_a_float_for_an_integer_conversion() Io -> () {
    ro f f64 = 2.5
    // nova:expect E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET -- `checked_neg` is a blanket bounded to `Ints` (was `try_to_i16`; renamed `to_i16` by D430 R7 2026-09-05, and R6 will make `f.to_i16()` LEGAL for floats, so the fixture uses a blanket that stays `Ints`-only), and `f64` is not a member; before the fix this line compiled and printed `true`
    println(f.checked_neg().is_some())
}
